markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Seeing the Filters

Filters shape what shows up in a feed, but they are not always easy to notice. In the last level, you compared two feeds and saw how filters can emphasize some ideas while leaving others out.

In this level, you’ll review a few key terms and think about how platforms can make filtering more transparent.

### **Terms to Know**

<iframe src="https://amandadennard-dotcom.github.io/Unit4_testing/vocab_flip_cards.html" width="100%" height="280" style="border:none;" title="Vocabulary flip cards"></iframe>

---

## Transparency Check: What would you want to see?

An <strong>active filter</strong> is a current rule being applied to decide what content appears, such as “Following,” “Education,” or “Last 7 days.”

A <strong>filter bubble</strong> can happen when a feed mostly shows content that matches what a user already likes, clicks, or agrees with. If the filters are hidden, users may not realize their view is being shaped.

Below are two versions of a feed. One shows the active filters. One does not.

👉 Which design feels more trustworthy? Why?

<div style="display:flex; gap:16px; flex-wrap:wrap; justify-content:center; margin-top:10px;">

  <div style="background:#ffffff; border:1px solid #ddd; border-radius:12px; padding:14px; width:340px;">
    <div style="font-size:12pt; font-weight:bold; margin-bottom:8px;">Version 1: Filters Shown</div>

  <div style="background:#dccbea; border:1px solid #8c52ba; border-radius:10px; padding:10px; font-size:12pt; margin-bottom:10px;">
      <strong>Active filters:</strong> Following • Education • Last 7 days<br>
      <span style="font-size:11pt;">You can edit these filters.</span>
    </div>

  <div style="background:#f5f5f5; border-radius:8px; padding:10px; font-size:11pt; margin-bottom:8px;">
      📘 New classroom tech tools for teachers
    </div>

  <div style="background:#f5f5f5; border-radius:8px; padding:10px; font-size:11pt;">
      🎓 Study tips from educators you follow
    </div>

  <div style="font-size:12pt; color:#444; margin-top:10px;">
      Users can see what is shaping the feed and change the filters if they want a different view.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="background:#ffffff; border:1px solid #ddd; border-radius:12px; padding:14px; width:340px;">
    <div style="font-size:12pt; font-weight:bold; margin-bottom:8px;">Version 2: Filters Hidden</div>

  <div style="background:#dccbea; border:1px solid #8c52ba; border-radius:10px; padding:10px; font-size:12pt; margin-bottom:10px;">
      <strong>For You</strong><br>
      <span style="font-size:11pt;">No explanation shown.</span>
    </div>

  <div style="background:#f5f5f5; border-radius:8px; padding:10px; font-size:11pt; margin-bottom:8px;">
      📘 More classroom tech tools you may like
    </div>

  <div style="background:#f5f5f5; border-radius:8px; padding:10px; font-size:11pt;">
      📘 Another post similar to what you clicked before
    </div>

  <div style="font-size:12pt; color:#444; margin-top:10px;">
      Users may not realize the feed is being filtered based on past behavior, which can create a filter bubble.
    </div>
  </div>

</div>

<br>

<details style="background-color:#ececec; padding:10px; margin-bottom:10px; border:1px solid transparent; border-radius:5px;">
  <summary style="font-size:12pt; font-weight:bold;">💡 Check your thinking:</summary>

  <div style="margin-top:20px; margin-left:20px; margin-right:20px; margin-bottom:40px;">
    <p style="font-size:12pt; line-height:1.45; margin:0;">
      Version 1 may feel more trustworthy because it clearly shows the active filters being used and gives users a way to change them. Version 2 may look simpler, but it hides how the feed is being shaped. When filters are hidden, users may think they are seeing a complete or neutral view, even though the algorithm is selecting content for them.
    </p>
  </div>
</details>



MARKDOWN
